meta {
  name: Calling without a session
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/mcp
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

body:json {
  {
    "jsonrpc": "2.0",
    "id": 25,
    "method": "tools/list"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 400
}

tests {
  test("400 Bad Request — no session id was sent", function() {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(400);
    expect(res.getBody().error.message).to.contain("Missing session ID");
  });

  test("the server offers a fresh session id it will not honour retroactively", function() {
    expect(res.getHeader("mcp-session-id")).to.be.a("string");
  });
}

docs {
  The only request in this collection with **no `mcp-session-id` header** — open its
  **Headers** tab and compare with any of its neighbours.

  MCP over HTTP is **stateful**. `initialize` mints a session; every later request has to
  echo it, and the server rejects anything that does not:

  | Sent | Answer |
  |---|---|
  | nothing | `400 Bad Request: Missing session ID` |
  | an id the server never issued | `404 Not Found` |
  | the id from `initialize` | the actual result |

  This is what makes the collection order load-bearing, and what makes MCP unlike the REST
  project next door — where every request stands alone and any of them can be run first.

  A closing note on the `404` row: it is also what a client sees after the **server
  restarts**. Sessions live in memory, so an id that worked a second ago becomes unknown,
  and the fix is to re-run *Handshake › Initialize* rather than to retry the call.
}
